			<div class="content" id="post-content-2164516">
									

<p>Offhand, fron reading the article, I'd say:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If the epidermis is technically dead skin, could 293's effect take place on your skin?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>No/very unlikely. It's still part of a living being.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If the you have a prosthetic, could you get attached to it?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sure.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If you leave two object's with 293's presence, would both of them get effected?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Possibly/probably. I don't think we have any good tests on it's transmission.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If so, would an effected person get attached to BOTH of them and think that they are trying to compete for attention?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think not. I suspect you'd fixate on just one item. No doubt the first few to be exposed to this had a number of items around to potentially fixate on.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Could you become obsessed with part of someone that isn't alive (like carotin or feces)?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;I have no idea. I want to say no, but I honestly don't know if it's logic or squick making me say that.</p>

